※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=89 diag=none

※ A pre-condition that does not hold stops the program with a status of its
※ own: it is the **caller** that was wrong, and a caller acting on the status
※ can tell "I called this wrongly" from "the thing I called is broken".
※
※ The value comes out of a variable, which is what keeps the compiler from
※ seeing the failure while compiling: one it can see is an error and not a
※ fault, by the rule arithmetic already follows.

let zero: u8 = 0u8

@[inline(never)]
fn twice(a: u8) → u8 pre(a > 0u8):
    a ⊞ a

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(twice(zero), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
